## Use clear language

Start a control label with a verb and name the object: **Turn off kitchen lights**, not **Off**. Use sentence case. Use “you” where it helps. Write “cannot” and “do not” in warnings. Do not use words such as “execute”, “initialise” or “entity service invocation” in household controls.

See [Write clear titles](../content/writing-guidelines/clear-titles.html), [Use familiar, concrete words](../content/writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-010) and [Use the right tone](../content/writing-guidelines/right-tone.html).

## Keep state and action apart

| Situation | State label | Action label |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Light is off | Off | Turn on kitchen light |
| Heating is requesting heat | Heating | Change temperature |
| Request sent, no reply yet | Turning on… | None. Disable the button only until the device replies. |
| No current reading | Unavailable | Try again, only if a retry is possible |
| No alerts | No current alerts | View history |

Show a missing reading as unavailable, not as zero. Do not say a request worked until the device reports it. Use the names the household chose for its devices. Do not rename them to suit a writing rule.

See [States, requests and errors](../content/writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html).

## Explain errors

Say what happened, then give one step that fits the cause. For example: “The kitchen light has not reported a new state. Check it before you send another request.” Put raw error details in a disclosure when they help with troubleshooting.

Do not invent a recovery step. If the interface cannot retry, say where the person can check the device.

## Put the important thing first

Lead with what the person needs. Keep headings short and specific. Use bullets for parallel choices and numbered steps for a sequence. See [Create a clear structure](../content/writing-guidelines/clear-structure.html).

## Write links and units

Describe the destination in link text: “View energy use”, not “Click here”. Keep each number with its unit and the period it covers. Use locale-aware formatting. Do not fix pounds, Celsius or a UK time zone over the household’s own settings. See [Add links](../content/writing-guidelines/add-links.html).

Write documentation in UK English. In the application, keep Home Assistant’s own translations. Do not replace them with fixed English labels.
